Z||000000||Formula||07-25-2000||10:57 PM||||On the way back from the western PA fiero club meet I just happened to pull up beside a new cougar.... I wasnt paying attention and it took off the line like a bat out of hell, but the fiero immediately cought up and passed it with ease..... I also just beat a 2000 Mustang, Whats up with these new fords not being able to beat my car. (I lost to a civic beofre when the brakes were hanging up, but now nothing beats me)||||reg||1
Z||000001||KnightRyder31||07-25-2000||11:23 PM||||Simply put: Its because you have a Fiero, and they dont.||||reg||6
Z||000002||ChadMan||07-26-2000||10:26 AM||||Nice kill, Formula.  One of my best friends has a 2000 Cougar: V6, 5-speed, black w/wing and polished alloy wheels.<P>He let me drive it, and I thought it was a really nice car, but it sure didn't feel like 170 hp.  The ass-o-meter said it was slower than any stick-shift V6 Fiero I've ever driven.  Are Cougars really heavy or something?||||reg||5
Z||000003||Fformula88||07-26-2000||11:49 AM||||For some reason, the Cougars are just dogs.  They are around 2900 Lbs curb wieght, so they are heavier than a Fiero by a bit.  The 5-spds are fairly quick, but those ATX Cougars sure don't feel like a V6 at all.  My 4 cyl Probe ATX is nearly as fast as the 6 cyl Cougar ATX.  Any Fiero would take it.  As for a 5-spd, it would be close...<P>Oh, I just had to add that they do have 170 HP engines, but that peak is around 6000 RPM.  Torque is 165 but somewhere after 4000 RPM.<p>[This message has been edited by Fformula88 (edited 07-26-2000).]||||reg||1
